An additional way to identify worthy/worthless gems is a 
engraving/chest-use method.

You surely know about engraving:
If you engrave with a gem and it reads:
>You engrave on the floor with a <xxx> gem....
it is worthy. Just 'C'all it "worthy <colour>".

If it reads:
>You write in the dust with a gem.
It can be a worthless or worthy one. 'C'all it "soft <colour> <nr>". 
If it is the first one of that colour, choose <nr> as 1, later 2 etc.

Now the part with the chest/boxes is coming in:
While kicking a chest there is chance that things made of glass 
in it do break. But wait... Aren't these worthless gems worthless 
pieces of GLASS?

So just put your soft gems in a (empty) chest, remember what gems 
you have put in there. (This is the place where the number and a 
sheet of paper could come in handy. :)

Than kick the box a dozen times. If there is no 'You hear a muffled 
shatter.' for a dozen times all left gems are worthy ones. Mark the 
gems you miss on your paper worthless (and if you see them next time, 
'C'all them worthless too.)

